<body>
<h1>Unit 26: Recent Trends in Physics</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Modern Physics</p><p class="summary">The final unit is about the edge of the subject: the Standard Model and where it stops, the detection of gravitational waves and neutrino oscillations, and the questions that a generation of experiments is now narrowing - slowly, and honestly.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>The Standard Model describes the particles and forces that have been confirmed in the laboratory, but it does not explain dark matter, dark energy, quantum gravity, or the cosmic matter-antimatter imbalance.</li><li>The LHCb collaboration at CERN observed CP violation in baryon decays - announced in March 2025 and published in Nature in July 2025 - adding a piece to the matter-antimatter puzzle, though the Standard Model's CP violation remains far too small to explain the observed imbalance (CERN).</li><li>The Higgs field gives mass to the W and Z bosons and to fermions through the Yukawa coupling, and the Higgs boson was observed at the LHC in 2012.</li><li>Neutrino oscillation proved that neutrinos have mass, which reshaped how the Standard Model is interpreted.</li><li>Gravitational waves were first detected in 2015 by LIGO, opening a new observational window on the universe.</li><li>Experiments in the frontier are statements of probability with controlled systematics: evidence means a statistically significant deviation, not proof.</li><li>The Standard Model accounts for three of the four forces and every confirmed particle, but not dark matter, dark energy, quantum gravity, or the matter-antimatter asymmetry.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>Every frontier claim carries an uncertainty; a null result constrains the strength of a coupling rather than proving absence.</li><li>The Standard Model is an effective description validated at the energies tested, and new physics could appear above them without invalidating it.</li><li>Numerical simulation is a third pillar of the subject, and it must be validated against analytically solvable limits and against experiment.</li><li>Observational limits and theoretical predictions live on different timescales; a bound announced by an experiment can tighten long before a theory explains it.</li><li>The Higgs field gives mass to the W and Z bosons and to the fermions, but most of the mass of everyday matter comes from the binding energy inside protons and neutrons, not from the Higgs.</li></ul><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Higgs boson mass</td><td>about 125.2 GeV</td></tr><tr><td>Gravitational wave detection (LIGO)</td><td>September 2015</td></tr><tr><td>KATRIN direct bound on the neutrino mass</td><td>0.45 eV/c^2</td></tr><tr><td>Distance to the first gravitational-wave detection, GW150914</td><td>about 1.3 billion light years</td></tr><tr><td>Age of the universe</td><td>about 13.8 billion years</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>Why is there far more matter than antimatter in the universe?</b></p><p class="status">Open question</p><p>CP violation is necessary but, in the Standard Model, far too small; mechanisms such as leptogenesis are candidate explanations under active study (CERN).</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>What drives the accelerating expansion of the universe?</b></p><p class="status">Open question</p><p>The observations are secure; the mechanism is not, and no confirmed detection of the responsible particle has been made.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Does gravity admit a consistent quantum description?</b></p><p class="status">Open question</p><p>No fully consistent theory exists, and the attempts to build one have not yet produced testable predictions.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Write down the particle content of the Standard Model and identify which observed phenomena it fails to explain.</li><li>Explain how the observation of neutrino oscillation implies a non-zero neutrino mass, and where the mass enters the oscillation formula.</li><li>Take one recent physics result and trace the chain from raw measurement through inference and model to the open question that remains.</li></ol><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://home.cern/new-piece-matter-antimatter-puzzle/">CERN: a new piece in the matter-antimatter puzzle</a></li><li><a href="https://www.energy.gov/science/np/articles/katrin-narrows-down-range-neutrinos-mass">US DOE: KATRIN narrows the range of the neutrino mass</a></li><li><a href="https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explainssuperconductivity">US DOE: Superconductivity explained</a></li><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/dark-matter/">NASA: Dark Matter</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/11-5-the-standard-model">OpenStax: The Standard Model</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/11-6-the-big-bang">OpenStax: The Big Bang</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
